      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Michel Della Negra",
        "Peter Jenni",
        "Tejinder Virdee"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Higgs boson discovery completed a central piece of the Standard Model and confirmed a mechanism with deep consequences for particle physics.",
        "attribution": "ATLAS and CMS achieved the discovery through enormous collaborations. These three leaders helped build and direct the experiments, though credit for the collective achievement cannot be confined neatly to a few people.",
        "maturity": "The signal was independently observed by both experiments in 2012 and has since been studied in detail.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The main difficulty is how to recognize a few individuals for work performed by two large collaborations."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Heino Falcke",
        "J. Anton Zensus",
        "Sheperd S. Doeleman"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Imaging the shadow of a supermassive black hole made a striking prediction of general relativity accessible to direct observation and demonstrated a new kind of global radio astronomy.",
        "attribution": "Falcke developed the shadow proposal, while Zensus and Doeleman were central to the observing effort and its leadership. The result depended on a broad international collaboration.",
        "maturity": "The first image was published in 2019, with later observations and images providing important follow-up.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The observed structure is an image of emission around the shadow rather than a direct image of the event horizon.",
          "Credit within the large collaboration requires careful assessment."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B061",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Charles L. Bennett",
        "David N. Spergel",
        "Lyman A. Page"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "WMAP's precise full-sky measurements transformed cosmology into a quantitatively constrained field and established a durable account of the universe's large-scale history.",
        "attribution": "Bennett led the mission, while Spergel and Page made major contributions to its scientific interpretation and measurement program. The mission's achievement was collective.",
        "maturity": "The results have been repeatedly checked against later observations, especially those from Planck, and remain foundational.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The division of credit among mission leadership, instrument teams, and the wider collaboration merits scrutiny."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B007",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Harald Rose",
        "Knut Urban",
        "Maximilian Haider"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Correcting spherical aberration overcame a fundamental limitation of electron microscopy and enabled much sharper imaging of atomic-scale structure.",
        "attribution": "Rose supplied key optical theory and design, while Urban and Haider helped realize and demonstrate the corrected microscope. The distinction between conception and experimental implementation is meaningful here.",
        "maturity": "The correction was demonstrated in working instruments and became a mature capability that has influenced materials research.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The relative weight of the theoretical design, instrument construction, and subsequent development needs careful historical evaluation."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B002",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Alexei Kitaev",
        "Andrew M. Steane",
        "Peter W. Shor"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum error correction showed that quantum computation can, in principle, tolerate noise, turning fragile quantum states into a plausible basis for scalable information processing.",
        "attribution": "Shor and Steane developed landmark error-correcting codes, while Kitaev's topological approach supplied a distinct and influential route to fault tolerance. The contributions are related but not identical.",
        "maturity": "The theory is well established and experimental demonstrations have advanced considerably, though large-scale fault-tolerant computation remains a work in progress.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "It is not yet clear which approaches will underpin practical large-scale devices.",
          "A prize could recognize the conceptual breakthrough before its ultimate technological reach is settled."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B043",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks enable extraordinary precision and open tests of fundamental physics through controlled comparisons of time and frequency.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye developed leading, complementary realizations of the clock concept and demonstrated its scientific potential.",
        "maturity": "The technique is experimentally mature and has achieved striking precision, while applications to redefining the second and new tests continue to develop.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field's most consequential applications may still be emerging."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B006",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Daniel J. Eisenstein",
        "Matthew Colless",
        "Shaun Cole"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Using baryon acoustic oscillations as a standard ruler provided a powerful, independent way to measure cosmic expansion and probe dark energy.",
        "attribution": "Eisenstein, Colless, and Cole were central to the surveys and analysis that established the method. Sunyaev's broader contributions to the physical framework deserve separate examination.",
        "maturity": "The standard-ruler signal has been measured by successive surveys and is now a routine tool in precision cosmology.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The measurement rests on a chain from early-universe physics to galaxy surveys, making individual credit less straightforward."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B034",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Immanuel Bloch",
        "Markus Greiner",
        "Tilman Esslinger"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Ultracold atoms in optical lattices made controlled quantum many-body systems experimentally accessible, allowing phenomena from condensed matter physics to be studied in a clean setting.",
        "attribution": "Bloch, Greiner, and Esslinger led influential demonstrations and experimental programs; Zoller supplied important theoretical foundations. Contributions across theory and experiment warrant careful comparison.",
        "maturity": "The platform has produced robust results over many years and remains active, with its value as a simulator still being established against problems that cannot be solved otherwise.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field's lasting scientific payoff as a route to new physical insight is not yet fully settled.",
          "The proposed share of credit between experimenters and theorists is debatable."
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
